package arbor:extensions@1.0.0;

// Geometry to draw a shader on.
//
// ## The smallest interface in the set, and the safest
//
// A mesh source imports nothing. No network, no secrets, no filesystem — it is a pure
// function from parameters to vertices, and it is deterministic. That makes it the one place
// where the sandbox costs nothing at all: there is no capability to weigh, only a contract.
//
// It is also the reason this exists as an interface rather than a table of primitives in
// Arbor. A shader is written against geometry — a hex tile, a ramp, a crystal habit — and a
// preview that can only offer a sphere is a preview of a different shader. The meshes that
// matter are the ones in somebody's own engine, which Arbor will never ship and should never
// need to see.
//
// ## Params as a schema, not as a struct
//
// `catalogue` returns a JSON Schema per mesh kind, and the host renders controls from it. So
// a package can expose `geode` with `facets`, `roughness` and `seed`, and the panel draws
// three controls without Arbor knowing what a geode is. The alternative — a fixed parameter
// record — would mean every new mesh with a new knob is a new version of this file.
//
// The schema is the same shape the MCP tool surface already uses for its arguments, and for
// the same reason: a description that lives beside the value cannot drift from it.
interface mesh-types {
  /// Triangles, indexed, in the layout a GPU wants.
  ///
  /// Flat lists of floats rather than a list of vertex records: a record per vertex would
  /// cross the component boundary once per vertex, and a mesh worth previewing has tens of
  /// thousands. Positions are xyz triples, normals xyz, uvs xy — so `positions.len()` is
  /// three times the vertex count and the three lists agree on it.
  record mesh-data {
    positions: list<f32>,
    /// Empty when the source does not compute them; the host derives flat normals instead.
    normals: list<f32>,
    /// Empty when the source has no parameterisation; the host binds zeroes.
    uvs: list<f32>,
    /// Triangle list. Empty means "the positions are already in draw order".
    indices: list<u32>,
  }

  record mesh-kind {
    /// Stable within the package. The host addresses a mesh as `<package>/<id>`, so two
    /// packages offering `cube` do not collide and neither has to know about the other.
    id: string,
    /// What the picker shows.
    label: string,
    /// One line under the label. Empty for the obvious ones.
    description: string,
    /// JSON Schema (draft 2020-12) for the object `build` accepts. `{}` for a mesh with no
    /// parameters, which is a schema that validates the empty object and needs no special
    /// case anywhere.
    params-schema: string,
  }
}

interface mesh-source {
  use mesh-types.{mesh-data, mesh-kind};

  /// Every mesh this package offers.
  ///
  /// Called once when the package is loaded, so it must not be expensive: a package that
  /// generated its meshes here to answer would generate all of them to answer one question.
  catalogue: func() -> list<mesh-kind>;

  /// Build one, from a JSON object matching its schema.
  ///
  /// `params` is `{}` for a mesh with no parameters. Errors are strings because there is
  /// nothing a caller does differently between "no such id" and "facets must be at least 3" —
  /// both are shown to the person who typed them.
  build: func(id: string, params: string) -> result<mesh-data, string>;
}

/// The world a mesh source exports.
///
/// `log` is imported so a generator can explain itself when a parameter combination produces
/// nothing; there is deliberately nothing else to import.
world mesh-source-world {
  import log;
  export mesh-source;
}
